From c2474ce4d31ed75254279ffa7db177277544506e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Fattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:53:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(android):=20browser#128=20M3=20=E2=80=94?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20report=20the=20compositor=20surface's=20true=20screen=20orig?=
 =?UTF-8?q?in?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the M3 seam: the weave geometry's origin was a hardcoded (0,0)
placeholder, correct only for a bottom-anchored full-width window. Measured on
NP02J: a freeform window at true origin (2137,84) wove with its interlace phase
referenced ~2137 px away, and an ordinary full-width window whose surface sat
60 px below the panel top anchored 60 rows off for minutes at a time.

The origin only exists in the Java UI — there is no window handle, and a pure
MOVE raises no resize — so, exactly as the seam comment prescribed:

  * DisplayXrWindowGeometry.java samples View.getLocationOnScreen() of the
    compositor SurfaceView on a Choreographer cadence. The SURFACE, not
    android.R.id.content: Chrome draws edge-to-edge, so the content view spans
    the whole panel (measured (0,0) 2560x1600) while the woven surface is the
    SurfaceView inset below the status bar ((0,60) 2560x1540). Change-gated
    with a ~1 s heartbeat so a GPU restart or a missed transition self-heals.
  * A new [EnableIf=is_android] SetWindowGeometry on the existing
    DisplayXRDisplayMode interface carries it browser -> GPU (same pipe as
    LocateViews, so the two stay ordered).
  * The GPU provider adopts the ORIGIN half only; the SIZE stays the root-pass
    size, which names the pixels actually being woven — pairing the true
    origin with the root-pass size is correct even when the two disagree
    mid-layout. Until the first report lands the geometry is bit-identical to
    the old placeholder.

Verified on NP02J end to end: the sampler reports (0,60) 2560x1540, the
runtime's window rect shows 0,60, and with the matched plug-in change
(leia-plugin: trust a nonzero reported y, no pre-applied inverse) the CNSDK
anchor computes 1600-1540-60 = 0 — correct — with zero weave failures.

MATCHED PAIR: a runtime whose Leia plug-in predates the trust change inverts
truthful origins into a fixed error (bottom-origin 0 where 60 is correct,
measured). Ship the plug-in first; an old browser reporting (0,0) keeps the
old compensation path bit-for-bit.

Per ADR-033 the browser reports geometry and NOTHING else; the weaver alone
owns phase and snapping.

OPTION 1 (detect-and-don't-weave): the Java feed also reports whether the
window's CONTAINER is scaling the surface (OEM freeform mini-window;
SurfaceFlinger tr=[0.67,0][0,0.67] measured on NP02J — window bounds exceed
the display's maximum metrics, the app-visible tell). A woven texture
resampled by any factor is a uniform double image no geometry can fix, so
while scaled the GPU provider's BatchMode() returns false: every tile routes
to the page-owned 2D path and the runtime's no-submit timeout returns the
panel to 2D. Unscaled again -> weaving resumes. A false positive (window
dragged off-edge unscaled) costs 2D content, never a broken weave.
